<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Overview of the tasks, showing the distinction between the 2 sentences of a minimal pair.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Tasks and examples of prompts</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The full list of prompts can be found in the project repository.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The task ’nonsense’ acts as sanity check for our experiments. It contains 250 minimal pairs where one sentence is full of made up words while the other is the same but contains one real world in a random position. One example of minimal pair is the following:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_p">‘af doi broiz oos thag plown’ 
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">‘af doi broiz <span id="A3.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">day</span> thag plown’</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Here we present also an example of a full prompt for this task:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_p">This sentence contains only nonsense words: ‘af doi broiz oos thag plown’. This sentence contains a real word: ‘af doi broiz <span id="A3.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">day</span> thag plown’.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">And an example of a reversed prompt:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A3.p7.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_p">This sentence contains a real word: ‘af doi broiz <span id="A3.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">day</span> thag plown’. This sentence contains only nonsense words: ‘af doi broiz oos thag plown’.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Next, we leverage the CrowS-pairs dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nangia et al. (2020)</a></cite>, which contains 1286 minimal pairs where one sentence shows a stereotype and the other a counter-stereotype. Here is an example of minimal pair from the dataset:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A3.p9.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_p">‘The <span id="A3.p9.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">poor</span> are really ignorant about how to handle the money they do have‘ 
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">‘The <span id="A3.p9.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">rich</span> are really ignorant about how to handle the money they do have‘</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Below is an example of a full prompt for the experiment:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A3.p11.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_p">This is a sentence that contains a stereotype: ‘The <span id="A3.p11.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">poor</span> are really ignorant about how to handle the money they do have’. This is a sentence that contains a counter-stereotype: ‘The <span id="A3.p11.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">rich</span> are really ignorant about how to handle the money they do have’.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The next dataset we use for our experiments is ’DUST’ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wildenburg et al. (2024)</a></cite> which contains 2,097 minimal pairs and several types of ambiguities like: Referential ambiguity, PP and VP attachment, Implicit reference and others…
Here is an example of a referential ambiguity minimal pair from the dataset:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A3.p13.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_p">’In the storm, the <span id="A3.p13.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">tree</span> fell down and crashed through the roof of my house. Now, I have to get <span id="A3.p13.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">it</span> removed.’ 
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">’In the storm, the <span id="A3.p13.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">trees</span> fell down and crashed through the roof of my house. Now, I have to get <span id="A3.p13.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">them</span> removed.’</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Below is an instance of its corresponding prompt:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A3.p15.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_p">This is an ambiguous sentence: ’In the storm, the <span id="A3.p15.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">tree</span> fell down and crashed through the roof of my house. Now, I have to get <span id="A3.p15.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">it</span> removed.’. This is an unambiguous sentence: ’In the storm, the <span id="A3.p15.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">trees</span> fell down and crashed through the roof of my house. Now, I have to get <span id="A3.p15.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">them</span> removed.’.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">And lastly, we collect 2 tasks from BLiMP: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Warstadt et al. (2023)</a></cite> anaphor gender agreement (1000 sentences) and animacy verification (1000 sentences). Here, we present an example of minimal pairs for anaphor gender agreement and animacy verification respectively:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A3.p17.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.p17.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p17.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Anaphor gender agreement:
<br class="ltx_break"></span>‘Katherine can’t help <span id="A3.p17.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">herself</span>’ 
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">‘Katherine can’t help <span id="A3.p17.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">himself</span>’</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A3.p18.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p18.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Animacy verification:
<br class="ltx_break"></span>‘Amanda was respected by some <span id="A3.p18.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">waitresses</span>’ 
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">‘Amanda was respected by some <span id="A3.p18.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">picture</span>’</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A3.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Below there are example prompts for both these minimal pairs:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p20" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A3.p20.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.p20.1.1" class="ltx_p">This is a grammatical sentence: ‘Katherine can’t help <span id="A3.p20.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">herself</span>’. This is its ungrammatical counterpart: ‘Katherine can’t help <span id="A3.p20.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">himself</span>’</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A3.p21" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A3.p21.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.p21.1.1" class="ltx_p">This is a meaningful sentence: ‘Amanda was respected by some <span id="A3.p21.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">waitresses</span>’. This is a nonsensical sentence: ‘Amanda was respected by some <span id="A3.p21.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">picture</span>’.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Tasks results</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Figure  <a href="#A4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Appendix D Tasks results ‣ Is my model perplexed for the right reason? Contrasting LLMs’ Benchmark Behavior with Token-Level Perplexity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the normalized proportion of the perplexity difference for the nonsense task. As expected from this sanity check, across all models, the pivotal tokens (i.e., the inserted real words) are the primary drivers of the perplexity shift, clearly detaching from the baseline of the other tokens. However, we can observe that the explanatory proportion of the pivotal tokens rarely reaches the maximum theoretical value of <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, reaching instead a maximum of <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 0.6</annotation></semantics></math>. This behavior hints that the baseline <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>PPL contribution for the median token might not be perfectly zero. A plausible explanation for this lies in the nature of the nonsense words themselves, because nonsense words are extremely rare tokens their baseline probabilities are inherently low and unstable. The insertion of a single real word might alter the context and the model’s internal representations just enough to perturb the probability distributions of the surrounding nonsense tokens, thus causing artificial thus causing artificial fluctuations in their token-level perplexity, which effectively inflates the total <math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>PPL denominator and shifts the median baseline away from zero.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29396v1/New_images/nonsense_words_stacked_barplot_log_differences_multiple_gemma_3_mistral_llama_qwen.png" id="A4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/386;" width="476" height="386" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Natural logarithm of the amount of sentences (y-axis) in the nonsense words task for which a proportion of the difference in perplexity is explained by key tokens (x-axis). We see that the pivotal tokens explain a bigger proportion of the difference in perplexity then other tokens and/or periods, although only a maximum of <math id="A4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 50\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the difference in perplexity is explained by these tokens.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29396v1/New_images/blimp_animacy_stacked_barplot_log_differences_multiple_gemma_3_mistral_llama_qwen.png" id="A4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/386;" width="476" height="386" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Natural logarithm of the amount of sentences (y-axis) in the BLiMP animacy task for which a proportion of the difference in perplexity is explained by key tokens (x-axis). We see that the pivotal tokens explain a bigger proportion of the difference in perplexity than other tokens, with only a small amount being explained by the periods. However, we can notice as also for this task the pivotal tokens are accountable for only a maximum of <math id="A4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 50\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the difference in perplexity.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29396v1/New_images/blimp_stacked_barplot_log_differences_multiple_gemma_3_mistral_llama_qwen.png" id="A4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/386;" width="476" height="386" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Natural logarithm of the amount of sentences (y-axis) in the BLiMP anaphor agreement task for which a proportion of the difference in perplexity is explained by key tokens (x-axis). We see that the pivotal tokens explain a greater proportion of the difference in perplexity than other tokens, but with a non-negligible amount being explained also by the periods. As for the previous task we can notice how the pivotal tokens accounts for only a maximum of <math id="A4.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 50\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the difference in perplexity.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29396v1/New_images/stereotypes_stacked_barplot_log_differences_multiple_gemma_3_mistral_llama_qwen.png" id="A4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/386;" width="476" height="386" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Natural logarithm of the amount of sentences (y-axis) in the stereotypes task for which a proportion of the difference in perplexity is explained by key tokens (x-axis). Here, we can notice how the pivotal tokens along with the periods explains the bigger proportion of the difference in perplexity. Again, as for the previous tasks these tokens accounts for only a maximum of <math id="A4.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 50\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the difference in perplexity.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29396v1/New_images/dust_stacked_barplot_log_differences_multiple_gemma_3_mistral_llama_qwen.png" id="A4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/386;" width="476" height="386" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Natural logarithm of the amount of sentences (y-axis) in the DUST task for which a proportion of the difference in perplexity is explained by key tokens (x-axis). For this task we can observe how pivotal tokens are almost entirely accountable for the difference in perplexity, also DUST is the only task where these tokens account for the entire difference.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</article>
